# GitHub App for app builds

With app builds, a site owner can set up, start and monitor their own AAPS build from
Nocturne. The build runs in the owner's private GitHub repository. The owner's workflow signs
the APK with the owner's own key. Nocturne connects to that repository through a GitHub App. Each
instance registers its own GitHub App. This page tells you how to do that.

If you do not register an App, app builds stay off. Nothing else in Nocturne changes.

## What the App can do

The App gets three repository permissions and no more:

| Permission | Access | Use |
|------------|--------|-----|
| **Secrets** | Read and write | Nocturne writes the signing-key secret. It sends only a value that the browser encrypted with the repository's public key. Nocturne cannot decrypt it. |
| **Actions** | Read and write | Nocturne starts the build workflow and reads the status of each build. |
| **Metadata** | Read-only | GitHub gives this permission to every App. |

The App does not get **Contents** or **Administration**. Thus Nocturne cannot change the
workflow file, read the code, or create a repository. The owner creates the repository from
the AAPS Builder template. Then the owner installs the App on that one repository.

## 1. Register the App

1. On GitHub, go to **Settings > Developer settings > GitHub Apps**. For an organization,
   go to the organization's **Settings > Developer settings > GitHub Apps**.
2. Click **New GitHub App**.
3. Fill in the fields:

| Field | Value |
|-------|-------|
| **GitHub App name** | A name that is unique on GitHub, for example `Nocturne builds (example.com)`. |
| **Homepage URL** | `https://example.com` |
| **Callback URL** | `https://example.com/api/v4/app-builds/github/callback` |
| **Request user authorization (OAuth) during installation** | On |
| **Expire user authorization tokens** | On |
| **Setup URL** | Leave empty. GitHub disables it when it requests user authorization during installation. It then sends the user to the callback URL. |
| **Webhook** | Off. Clear **Active**. |
| **Repository permissions** | **Secrets**: Read and write. **Actions**: Read and write. **Metadata**: Read-only. |
| **Account permissions** | None. |
| **Where can this GitHub App be installed?** | **Any account**. |

Replace `example.com` with the domain in `BASE_DOMAIN`. Use the apex domain, not a site
subdomain. Nocturne sends each callback to the site that started it.

4. Click **Create GitHub App**.

<Callout type="warning" title="Do not add permissions">

  Nocturne needs only the three permissions above. Do not give the App more. Each owner sees
  these permissions when they install the App.

</Callout>

## 2. Get the keys

On the page of the new App:

1. Copy the **Client ID**.
2. Click **Generate a new client secret**. Copy the secret. GitHub shows it only one time.
3. Under **Private keys**, click **Generate a private key**. GitHub downloads a `.pem` file.
4. Copy the App name from the App's public page URL, `https://github.com/apps/<name>`.

## 3. Configure Nocturne

Add these variables to the environment of the `nocturne-api` service:

| Variable | Value |
|----------|-------|
| `GitHub__BuildApp__Slug` | The App name from the public page URL. |
| `GitHub__BuildApp__ClientId` | The Client ID. |
| `GitHub__BuildApp__ClientSecret` | The client secret. |
| `GitHub__BuildApp__PrivateKey` | The `.pem` file. See below. |

The private key file has many lines. Many environment files accept only one line for each
value, for example a Docker Compose `.env` file. For those, give the key as one line of base64:

```bash
base64 -w0 your-app.private-key.pem
```

Nocturne accepts the base64 text or the full `.pem` text with its `BEGIN` and `END` lines.

Restart the API. App builds stay off until you set all four variables. If Nocturne cannot read the
private key, app builds also stay off. The API log then shows a warning that names
`GitHub:BuildApp:PrivateKey`. The warning does not show the key.

<Callout type="warning" title="Keep the keys secret">

  The private key and the client secret let a person act as your App. Keep them out of anything
  you share. If you lose one, delete it on the App page and make a new one.

</Callout>

## What Nocturne keeps

For each site, Nocturne keeps the installation number, the GitHub account name, the
repository name and who connected it. It also keeps the names of the repositories in the
installation that this person administers. Nocturne does not keep a GitHub token. It gets a
short-lived installation token from GitHub when it needs one. Nocturne does not keep the
token that GitHub gives during installation. It uses that token to find the installation and
the repositories that the user administers, then revokes it.

The built APK stays in the owner's repository. The owner downloads it from the build page on
GitHub. Nocturne does not get or keep the APK.

Only a site owner, or a member who manages site settings, can use app builds. The person who
connects the repository does so with their own GitHub account. Only that person can choose the
repository, save the signing key and start a build. That person must be an administrator of the
repository on GitHub. Other site owners can see the status and the builds, and they can disconnect.
To take over, another owner connects again with their own GitHub account.

## Remove the App

When an owner disconnects app builds, Nocturne forgets the connection and shows a link to the
installation on GitHub. The owner removes the App there. To stop app builds for all sites,
remove the four variables and restart the API.
